{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous object-cleaning demos: session note

When several differently sized grids contain solid monochrome rectangles among sparse alien-colored marks, don't infer a simple global recoloring from palette overlap. Compare each pair's deltas and classify objects: the demos can show preservation of intact rectangles while isolated noise is erased and intrusions inside a target object are repaired. Record separately (a) isolated marks cleared to the background, (b) interior foreign-color cells replaced by the enclosing object's color, (c) genuine missing cells filled to complete a rectangle, and (d) non-target/background regions left unchanged. Do not automatically fill every connected component's bounding box: validate each proposed target's original geometry and its output mask. The common operation may be shape- or context-selected rather than applying to every colored cell.

After a wrong answer, reject the entire selection/repair hypothesis and replay all demos; changing only the set of speckles or rectangle bounds is not sufficient. A new paid example is worthwhile only when surviving selection rules predict different test outputs. Mechanically generate and validate the output grid before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-structured-motif-reassessment", "file": "references/session-285c935d-review.md", "content": "# Session review: stripes versus a sparse diagonal test motif

The stream supplied three demonstrations. In the 8×8 pair, rows of color 3 remained, rows 2 and 6 became 7, while rows with colors 8 and 7 were retained. The 15×15 pair replaced zero rows with 4 except where some rows instead showed a repeated 5,3,7,2 sequence; existing stripe colors mostly remained. The 4×4 pair changed only the first row from 0 to 9, leaving 4,2,9 rows unchanged. These examples do not support a single simple diagonal-period fill, and the third demo contradicts treating every zero cell/row uniformly. The submitted periodic diagonal projection was incorrect.

Reusable review lesson: after a wrong projection, do not preserve the assumed period/orientation and make a local adjustment. Reconstruct all demonstrations and distinguish a row/sequence transformation from a rule conditioned on stripe arrangement or marker geometry. In particular, a structured test with diagonal tracks must be analyzed independently at coordinate level; do not assign colors by diagonal phase unless that exact relation explains every demo and preserves/recolors markers consistently. If remaining candidates differ on the test, use another demo only if it can discriminate them; otherwise choose a fully evidenced prediction rather than a speculative pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-structured-motif-reassessment/references/session-285c935d-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/post-error-rule-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassess after an incorrect ARC submission

A wrong submission is evidence against the transformation hypothesis as a whole. Do not respond by changing a few guessed cells while preserving an unverified premise. Re-read the full demonstrations and list plausible rule families, then compare complete-grid predictions coordinate-by-coordinate, including untouched background, marker retention, orientation, and extent. If the remaining hypotheses predict different outputs on a new example, spend a demo request; otherwise submit only the best-supported complete prediction.

For sparse puzzles combining a motif and separate marker cells, explicitly test whether the motif is geometrically transformed around/at markers (reflection, rotation, projection) in addition to translation or replication. Validate the candidate transformation against all demo coordinates before extrapolating.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/post-error-rule-reassessment.md"}
